<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Varna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1444">1444</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade-of-varna">Crusade of Varna</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> was fought on 10 November 1444 near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> port of Varna in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, where the Ottoman army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> defeated a crusading army of Hungarians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Wallachians. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-iii-of-poland">Vladislaus I of Hungary</a> (Władysław III of Poland) was killed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> escaped. The defeat ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade-of-varna">Crusade of Varna</a> and western hopes of driving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-varna.jpg" alt="Battle of Varna" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Matejko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Varna_1444.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Hungarian kingdom fell into crisis after the death of King Sigismund in 1437, and his successor Albert died in 1439. Hungarian nobles then called the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-iii-of-poland">Władysław III of Poland</a> to the throne, expecting help against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and he ruled beside the nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>. The sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> signed a ten-year truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and in 1444 resigned the throne to his twelve-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>.</p><p>Cardinal Julian Cesarini insisted that Władysław should break the truce, arguing that it was invalid because it had been made with infidels. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a> to raise a new crusader army under Hunyadi and Władysław. Çandarlı Halil Pasha recalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>, who commanded the Ottoman army. The crusaders numbered some 20,000, mainly Hungarian, Polish, Bohemian and 4,000 Wallachian troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>An Ottoman army of around 40,000 or 60,000 approached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> on 9 November. Cesarini first urged withdrawal, then a defence behind the Hussite Wagenburg until the fleet arrived. The young Władysław and Hunyadi rejected defensive tactics, and Hunyadi declared: &quot;To escape is impossible, to surrender is unthinkable.&quot;</p><p>On 10 November Hunyadi drew up the army in an arc 3.5 kilometres long between Lake Varna and the Franga plateau. Ottoman light cavalry assaulted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, and when the Christians pursued them in disorder, the Anatolian cavalry ambushed them. The right wing fled into the marshland, where Cesarini met his end. Against Hunyadi's advice, Władysław led 500 Polish knights at the Ottoman centre and almost reached Murad, but in front of his tent the king's horse fell or was stabbed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Hunyadi organised the retreat of the remains of the army, which had suffered thousands of casualties. The king's head and body were never found. Turkish chroniclers say a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> beheaded him, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>'s own memorandum says he survived the battle and was executed later in captivity. Hunyadi became Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> on 5 June 1446.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> passed to the four-year-old Ladislaus the Posthumous, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> had a three-year interregnum before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a>. The Ottoman victories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-kosovo">Second Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1448 deterred European states from sending help to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 1453. The fallen king was given the name Ladislaus of Varna (Władysław III Warneńczyk), and the battle is commemorated on the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Varna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sigismund's crusading army was crushed at the Battle of Nicopolis in 1396, and King Vladislaus I fell at the <b>Battle of Varna</b> in 1444.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Military</a></li></ul></section>
